🎯 The Big Picture First

All THREE are about one class HOLDING A REFERENCE to another class (unlike Inheritance which is "IS-A"). The difference between them is just: how STRONGLY are they tied together, and can one survive without the other? That's literally the entire concept. Let's rank them from WEAKEST bond to STRONGEST bond. 💪

Association  →  Aggregation  →  Composition
   (weakest)     (medium)         (strongest)
1️⃣ Association — "Just Acquaintances" 🤝

The rule: Two classes are simply RELATED/connected somehow, but there's NO ownership at all. Both objects live their OWN independent lives — they just happen to interact.

Real analogy: You and your college professor 🧑‍🏫. You're "associated" — you attend their class, they teach you — but neither of you OWNS the other. If you drop the course, your professor still exists, teaching other students. If the professor leaves, you still exist, just take a different course. Completely independent existence, just a relationship.

java
class Student {
    String name;
}

class Professor {
    String name;

    void teach(Student student) {  // just USES a Student temporarily, doesn't own it
        System.out.println(name + " is teaching " + student.name);
    }
}

Notice: Professor doesn't STORE a Student inside itself permanently — it just interacts with one temporarily through a method. Neither controls the other's existence. That's association — the loosest possible connection. 🔗

🎤 One-liner: "Association is a general relationship where two independent classes interact with each other, with no ownership implied."

2️⃣ Aggregation — "Has-A, But Can Survive Alone" 👨‍👩‍👧

The rule: One class HOLDS/OWNS another class as a member variable (real HAS-A relationship now), BUT the "owned" object can EXIST INDEPENDENTLY even if the owner is destroyed. Weak ownership.

Real analogy: A College and its Teachers 🏫. The College "has" teachers — sure, sounds like ownership. BUT if the college shuts down tomorrow, do the teachers just... cease to exist?? NO! They can go join ANOTHER college. The teachers existed BEFORE this college, and will exist AFTER, independent of it. That's the key giveaway of aggregation — the "part" survives without the "whole."

java
class Teacher {
    String name;
    Teacher(String name) { this.name = name; }
}

class College {
    String collegeName;
    List<Teacher> teachers; // College HAS teachers

    College(String collegeName, List<Teacher> teachers) {
        this.collegeName = collegeName;
        this.teachers = teachers; // teachers were created OUTSIDE, just passed in
    }
}

// Usage
Teacher t1 = new Teacher("Mr. Sharma");
Teacher t2 = new Teacher("Ms. Verma");

College college = new College("Pillai HOC", Arrays.asList(t1, t2));

// If college object gets destroyed, t1 and t2 STILL EXIST independently! ✅

The dead giveaway in code: the Teacher objects were created SEPARATELY (new Teacher(...)) OUTSIDE the College class, and just PASSED IN through the constructor. College doesn't control their lifecycle — it just holds references to already-existing objects. 🔑

🎤 One-liner: "Aggregation is a HAS-A relationship where the contained object can exist independently of the container — a weak form of ownership, often represented as a hollow diamond in UML."

3️⃣ Composition — "Has-A, Can't Survive Alone" 💔

The rule: One class OWNS another class SO STRONGLY that the "owned" object CANNOT exist without the "owner." If the owner is destroyed, the owned object dies WITH it. Strong ownership.

Real analogy: A House and its Rooms 🏠. Can a "Room" exist floating in space, completely independent of a house? NO! The moment the house is demolished, the rooms are GONE too — they were never meant to exist separately. The room's entire lifecycle is TIED to the house's lifecycle. That's composition — the strongest possible bond.

java
class Room {
    String type;
    Room(String type) {
        this.type = type;
        System.out.println(type + " room created 🏗️");
    }
}

class House {
    private List<Room> rooms; // House OWNS rooms completely

    House() {
        rooms = new ArrayList<>();
        rooms.add(new Room("Bedroom"));  // 🔑 Room is created INSIDE House itself!
        rooms.add(new Room("Kitchen"));  // House controls its ENTIRE lifecycle
    }
}

// Usage
House house = new House();
// The Rooms were born WITH the house, and if house gets garbage collected, rooms go too

The dead giveaway in code: the Room objects are created INSIDE the House constructor itself (new Room(...) happens WITHIN House), not passed in from outside. House fully controls when Rooms are born AND when they die. That's composition. 💀

🎤 One-liner: "Composition is a strong HAS-A relationship where the contained object's lifecycle is completely dependent on the container — if the container is destroyed, so is the contained object. Represented as a filled diamond in UML."